From 2e311c534703a5f3f3227c01573b2dfbd5698b53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 18:06:29 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 12/18] hwmon: macsmc: Unlock fan control on firmware that
 keeps fans in system mode

On the M4 Pro MacBook Pro (J616s) both fans start in mode 3, the
firmware's own fan policy, and the SMC refuses the manual mode write
of the fan_control path with an error, so fan control from Linux did
nothing. Public reports for M4 describe the same refusal and the Ftst
key that releases the fans to the OS.

When userspace first asks for a target on a fan whose mode write is
refused while it reads mode 3, set Ftst, wait for the fan to leave
system mode and ask again. When the last manual fan goes back to
auto, clear Ftst and wait for every fan to return to mode 3. Firmware
that takes the mode write directly keeps the mode 0 restore; firmware
that refuses it without an Ftst key gets the error as before.

Hand every manual fan back on unbind or remove, at shutdown and before
suspend, and whenever a write in the sequence fails. Targets are now
clamped to the fan's minimum and maximum instead of refused, so a
target can never go below the minimum. Everything stays behind the
existing fan_control parameter; without it nothing is written.

The new KUnit suite models locked and legacy firmware.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
---
 drivers/hwmon/Kconfig             |   7 +-
 drivers/hwmon/macsmc-hwmon-test.c | 262 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 drivers/hwmon/macsmc-hwmon.c      | 261 +++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 3 files changed, 497 insertions(+), 33 deletions(-)

